---
title: Overview
summary: Testing for ogygia. The devtools and the SSR profiler as test primitives, so your Playwright tests can measure hydration, interactions, server time and page weight, and fail when a budget is missed.
related:
  - testing/guide
  - devtools/overview
  - devtools/profiler/overview
---

A performance problem that you find by hand in the devtools tends to come back a few releases later, because nothing checks for it. `@ogygia/testing` lets your tests use what the [devtools](/docs/devtools/overview) and the profiler measure. Your tests click through the app as they already do, ogygia measures what happened, and the test asserts on the result.

It is a test helper, not a plugin: nothing goes in `vite.config.ts` for it. It gives you four primitives on an `ogygia` fixture:

| Primitive         | What it measures                                                                                        |
| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `page()`          | The page load as the browser measured it: vitals, each island's wake, and the dock's Page tab findings. |
| `hydration()`     | Each island's hydration status, with the difference for one whose markup changed.                       |
| `record(actions)` | What your actions did: slow interactions, dead clicks, requests, long tasks, shifts and errors.         |
| `profile(path)`   | The SSR profiler's report for a path: score, forecast, findings and weight.                             |

## Install

```bash
pnpm add -D @ogygia/testing @playwright/test
```

The app under test lists `testing()` from `@ogygia/testing/vite`, and `devtools({ profiler: true })` from [`@ogygia/devtools`](/docs/devtools/overview) when you use `profile()`. That is the dev server, or a preview build with them listed.

## A first test

ogygia does not import Playwright itself, so you hand it your own `test` and `expect`:

```ts title=tests/fixtures.ts
import { test as base, expect as baseExpect } from '@playwright/test';
import { withOgygia } from '@ogygia/testing';

export const { test, expect } = withOgygia(base, baseExpect);
```

```ts title=tests/checkout.spec.ts
import { test, expect } from './fixtures';

test('checkout stays fast', async ({ page, ogygia }) => {
  await page.goto('/cart');
  expect(await ogygia.page()).toMeetBudget({ lcp: 2500, cls: 0.1 });
  expect(await ogygia.hydration()).toHydrateCleanly();

  const session = await ogygia.record(() => page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Buy' }).click());
  expect(session).toMeetBudget({ maxInteractionMs: 200, errors: 0 });
});
```

## With other packages

**[@ogygia/devtools](/docs/devtools/overview)** measures the same things in the dock, and its profiler is what `profile()` reads. List `testing()` for the app under test in the same mode switch you already use, add the profiler when you use `profile()`, and give the fixture the profiler's key on a build:

```ts title=vite.config.ts
plugins: [
  ogygia(),
  ...(process.env.E2E ? [testing(), devtools({ dock: false, profiler: true })] : []),
  sveltekit()
]
```

```ts title=playwright.config.ts
export default defineConfig({
  use: {
    baseURL: 'http://127.0.0.1:4173',
    ogygiaOptions: { profilerKey: process.env.OGYGIA_PROFILER_SECRET }
  }
});
```

**`ogygia`'s regions** are what `hydration()` and `record()` report on: each island by name and instance (`Carousel#2`), with `t0` and `done`, the times it began to wake and could respond. A single report therefore tells you which step of a session woke an `interaction` island.

**[@ogygia/cli](/docs/cli/overview)** covers the same ground without a test suite: `npx ogygia perf` judges a list of pages against a budget file in CI.

